{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789842916,"nonce":"AFC1YdUvJLlczT5PbW7YMBGKYTk=","title":"A program that compiles a second module while it runs got the wrong code","message":"A Lua load compiles a chunk from inside the running program. Four things went wrong for a module compiled after another: the Cranelift backend defined a global both modules link (the library's) twice and refused the duplicate; the tiered backend replaced the function entries and bead ids of the earlier module's functions, so stubs handed out before named beads the new lazy compiler did not know, and a stub made afterwards for an earlier function fell back to the last compiled bead and ran the loaded chunk instead; the lazy compiler's scratch module assumed one module; and compile_module_entered took the interpreter's lock, which the running program holds, so the compile deadlocked.","files":null}]}